# What are the two types of "assertions" in Python?
# A1: 'assert' statements and 'assert' expressions

# If we encounter an "assert" statement, what will python execute?
# A: Python will execute the statement and if it is False, then it will throw an AssertionError

# When you’ve written an "assert" statement and executed the code, it succeeds. Why is that?
# A: Beacause the condition after the "assert" keyword is True. In Python, the "assert" keyword lets you test if a condition in your code returns True, if not, the program will raise an AssertionError with an optional error message.

# How can we prevent an AssertionError?
# A: We can prevent AssertionError by ensuring that the condition being tested is True at the time of the test, or by including an optional error message in the assertion.

# How can you see if an AssertionError was raised?
# A: You can use the "try/except" structure to catch an AssertionError if it does not cause your program to terminate unexpectedly.


# I learned that this code doesn’t terminate with an AssertionError and what was the advantage of using this “assert” feature in Python?
# A: The advantage of the “assert” statement in Python is that it allows developers to embed "assertions" in their programs. These assertions are used to debug the program. They work by checking conditions and in the case if the condition does not meet. The program throws an AssertionError and if the program is run in optimized mode (python -o), then the assertions are stripped out. This means that the assertions are disabled and running the Python program in this mode is faster.

# What happens in the case of the AssertionError in the following program?
# A: The program will stop running and throw an AssertionError. If there is a try/except block within the program to catch this exception, then the following message can be printed. If there are no try/except blocks, then the program will stop running with an error message that an AssertionError was thrown.

# How can we see what kind of AssertionError was thrown?
# A: By catching the AssertionError with the try/except block, you can see what kind of AssertionError was thrown and what the error message may be.
